[sai-00193] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires a time-constrained performance or presentation (e.g., 45-minute set list), explicitly include a 'Runtime and Logistics' section. This section must detail the estimated duration of each segment, the total runtime, and any mandatory statements regarding rehearsal accessibility, content warnings (e.g., curse words), or context-setting requirements requested by the client.
[sai-00194] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When tasked with creating clinical care plans or compliance-based medical documentation, perform a 'Clinical Requirement-to-Section' mapping before drafting. Create a table that lists every rubric-mandated diagnosis, assessment, and intervention (e.g., hand hygiene, aseptic technique, vital sign frequency, reassessment intervals). Do not begin drafting until every rubric item is assigned to a specific section, and perform a final 'Compliance Audit' to verify that all mandatory diagnoses (e.g., 'Risk for Infection') and clinical protocols are explicitly included in the text.
[sai-00196] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When tasked with data-heavy analysis from a provided dataset, perform a 'Data Integrity Audit' before drafting. Explicitly count the unique values for all required categories (e.g., regions, product types) in the source data and compare this count against the rubric's requirements. If the counts do not match, re-scan the dataset to identify missing records. Do not begin drafting until the data is fully aggregated and verified, ensuring that every rubric-mandated section (e.g., executive summary, regional breakdown) is populated with calculated, not hallucinated, figures.
[sai-00197] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For tasks requiring both data aggregation and document generation (e.g., slide decks, reports), implement a 'Calculation-to-Content' lock. Perform all mathematical operations (sums, averages, top-selling identification) in a scratchpad or code interpreter first. Only after these figures are verified against the rubric should you map them to the final document structure. This prevents the common mistake of drafting narrative content that contradicts the underlying data.
[sai-00199] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: For financial reporting tasks involving multi-period comparisons (e.g., YTD current vs. prior year) and projections, perform a 'Data-First' analysis: 1) Load the source data into a master dataframe. 2) Calculate YTD totals for both periods using explicit date filters. 3) Compute projections based on the specific historical quarters requested. 4) Conduct a 'Granular Field Audit' to map every rubric-mandated metric (e.g., dollar difference, percentage change) to a specific cell in the output. Do not draft the summary until these calculations are verified against the source data.
[sai-00201] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides data and requests a deliverable (e.g., a spreadsheet), adopt an 'Analyst-First' persona. Do not provide template code or placeholders. Instead, use the Python code interpreter to: 1) Extract all data points from the context, 2) Perform all required calculations (e.g., tiered pricing, margins, totals) within the script, and 3) Generate the final, fully-populated binary file as the primary deliverable.
[sai-00204] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires multiple, interdependent binary deliverables (e.g., an Excel correlation matrix and a PDF analysis report), treat the task as a unified data-engineering project. Use the Python code interpreter to generate the Excel file first, ensuring all calculations are programmatic. Then, use the same Python environment to generate the PDF report, ensuring that all analytical findings, coefficients, and data-driven conclusions are pulled directly from the generated Excel data structures to guarantee 100% consistency between the two files.
[sai-00205] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When generating professional financial reports, explicitly include metadata in the deliverable (e.g., return convention, observation count, methodology, and data source citations). Do not rely on text-based simulations; embed these fields programmatically into the generated binary files (e.g., as a 'Metadata' tab in Excel or a header section in the PDF) to ensure the deliverable meets professional audit standards.
[sai-00207] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides a technical standard (e.g., IPC-A-610G) as a reference for an SOP, perform a 'Standard-to-Requirement' extraction before drafting. Create a table that maps specific technical clauses from the standard (e.g., ESD definitions, equipment verification intervals, signage requirements) to the corresponding sections of your SOP. Do not rely on general knowledge of the standard; treat the provided document as the mandatory 'Source of Truth' and ensure every technical parameter is explicitly defined and integrated into the procedural steps.
[sai-00209] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For complex accessibility utilities (e.g., WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages), treat the 'live region' as a state management problem rather than a static DOM element. Implement a React Context or custom hook to manage a queue of messages, ensuring that rapid or overlapping updates are processed sequentially without interruption. Do not rely on simple component rendering; orchestrate the timing and sequence of announcements to ensure accessibility compliance.
[sai-00210] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a rubric for a technical utility mandates specific testing patterns (e.g., Sinon fake timers for time-dependent behavior), treat these as mandatory architectural requirements. Before drafting code, perform a 'Rubric-to-Test-Suite' mapping to ensure the proposed solution includes the necessary infrastructure (e.g., Sinon, Jest, or specific configuration files) to verify the utility's functional requirements under stress.
[sai-00213] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When tasked with financial reporting, amortization schedules, or GL reconciliations, treat the task as a data-engineering project. Use the Python code interpreter to load source files, perform row-level calculations, and generate a multi-tab .xlsx binary file. Do not provide text-based simulations or Markdown tables; the primary deliverable must be the binary file containing all required tabs, formulas, and reconciliation checks.
[sai-00216] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When creating compliance checklists, treat every rubric-mandated administrative field (e.g., Assessor Name, Workstation Location, Date) and response mechanism (e.g., Yes/No/NA checkboxes) as a mandatory functional requirement. Before drafting, create a 'Requirement-to-Section' mapping table to ensure these fields are present in every document. For multi-document tasks, perform a 'Cross-Document Synchronization Audit' to ensure that data fields (e.g., employee details) and checklist items are consistent across all deliverables.
[sai-00217] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires the use of a specific source (e.g., NIH ergonomics checklist), explicitly cite the source in the document header or footer. Treat the source as the 'Source of Truth' for content, but ensure all rubric-mandated administrative fields and formatting constraints are layered on top of the source material to ensure 100% compliance.
[sai-00218] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requests complex, file-based deliverables (e.g., audio tracks, synthesized music, or multi-file archives), treat the task as a technical engineering operation. Use the Python code interpreter to perform the actual data processing, synthesis, or file manipulation (e.g., using 'librosa', 'numpy', 'pydub', or 'zipfile'). Never provide 'placeholder' scripts or mock code that the user is expected to run; the primary deliverable must be the generated binary file itself, provided as a downloadable artifact.
[sai-00220] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When tasked with high-stakes negotiation or strategy documents, treat the prompt's rubric as a 'Compliance Checklist.' Before drafting, create a 'Requirement-to-Section' mapping table that assigns every rubric-mandated item (e.g., negotiation levers, contingency actions, transition milestones, numeric ranges for ZOPA) to a specific section header. Perform a final 'Compliance Audit' to verify that every item is addressed with granular, actionable detail rather than high-level narrative summaries.
[sai-00222] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When tasked with creating bilingual or multilingual forms, perform a 'Parity Audit' before drafting. Create a mapping table that lists every rubric-mandated field, header, and instruction in both languages. Ensure that the structural layout (e.g., table columns, blank rows for manual entry, 'Yes/No' checkboxes) is identical across both versions to maintain consistent data collection standards regardless of the client's language.
[sai-00223] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When designing forms for manual staff use, treat 'blank rows' and 'Notes/Comments' sections as mandatory functional requirements. Before drafting, explicitly map these structural elements to the rubric's requirements for tracking and follow-up. Ensure that every table includes sufficient blank rows for data entry and a dedicated 'Notes' section to capture qualitative follow-up information, as these are critical for compliance-driven document creation.
[sai-00224] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When tasked with regulatory or compliance-based audits, perform a 'Compliance-to-Rubric' mapping before drafting. Create a table that explicitly lists every constraint (e.g., specific document headers, required citation dates, regulatory paragraph numbers, and mandatory terminology) and maps each to a specific section of the output. Do not begin drafting until this mapping is complete, and perform a final 'Compliance Audit' to verify that every rubric-mandated detail is explicitly included in the final text.
[sai-00225] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt provides a specific regulatory source (e.g., VA Servicer Handbook M26-4), treat it as the exclusive 'Source of Truth.' Before drafting, extract the exact language and requirements from the provided source. Do not rely on internal knowledge of general servicing; instead, perform a 'Requirement-to-Section' mapping to ensure that every test question and exception statement is grounded in the specific regulatory language and citation requirements provided in the prompt.
[sai-00226] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires both data analysis and the generation of a binary file (e.g., .pptx, .xlsx), treat the task as a two-phase engineering operation. Phase 1: Use the code interpreter to load the dataset, perform all required calculations, and validate the results against the rubric. Phase 2: Use the code interpreter to generate the binary file using appropriate libraries (e.g., python-pptx, openpyxl), ensuring all rubric-mandated charts, tables, and formatting are programmatically defined. Do not attempt to simulate the file or perform calculations manually; the final output must be the generated binary file.
[sai-00228] helpful=1 harmful=0 :: When a prompt requires complex visual formatting (e.g., centered headers, image overlays, or specific subtitle placement) in a text-only output, use markdown syntax to simulate the layout. Use '<center>' tags for titles, explicit '[Image Overlay: [Description]]' placeholders for visual elements, and clear, hierarchical spacing to ensure the text is 'document-ready' for immediate copy-pasting into a layout application.
